  "createdAt": "2026-10-24T02:39:05.602Z",
  "description": "Broad strap leaflets instead of needle-thin ones (user feedback: the leaves read like pine needles). Each leaflet (pinna) is still one four-triangle strip, but its full width is now 10 cm (was 5 cm at a single widest point), on the same length (0.72 m at its longest): a narrow folded base at the rachis, full width at 16 % of its length, 86 % width at 64 %, then an acuminate tip, with a shallow V channel (edges 0.16 x width above the midrib), about 8:1 long to wide instead of 18:1. Width also scales with the leaflet's length along the frond, so base and tip leaflets are narrower. Same 24 leaflets per side; with the new width, neighbours overlap along the rachis and the crown reads as lush leaves from below and far away. Leaflets are held flatter on upper fronds (droop 16-42 deg plus 22 deg toward the frond tip, was 32-52 plus 18), angled 34-50 deg from perpendicular (was 44-62), rachis base radius 4 cm (was 3.4 cm). The dead frond in crown_husk uses the same leaflet build at 0.7 x width. The frond strip's edge bands are a little less dark and the midrib stripe softer. The crown random stream is preserved: the old leaflet draws are replayed and new leaflets use their own per-frond stream, so trunk (measured unchanged), frond arcs, coconuts and the dead frond rachis keep their places. Contracts kept: three parts (trunk, crown_husk, fronds), one PalmFronds material with the 256x4 band texture, TEXCOORD_0.x selects the band, TEXCOORD_0.y is the wind weight (0 at the crown, 1 at the tip; correlation with distance 0.96). 3,886 triangles (was 3,854).",
